- **Purpose.** One knob, $\sigma$, drives every measure at once, so you can see how the measures move together while still answering different questions:
  - the [Lorenz Curve](note:lorenz-curve) bows further below the 45° line (element `equality`);
  - the [Gini Coefficient](note:gini-coefficient) $G$ rises;
  - the [Percentile Ratios](note:percentile-ratios) widen;
  - the [Variance of Log Income](note:variance-of-log-income) is exactly $\sigma^2$;
  - the [top 10% share](note:top-income-shares) rises.
- **Scale invariance.** Raising $\mu$ by 1 multiplies every wage by $e \approx 2.72$. The density stretches to the right and the mean and median grow, but the Lorenz curve and every inequality measure stay put. The tests check this at $\mu = 1$ and $\mu = 5$.
